### Concept Notes: Resource Leases, Time-outs, and Compensating Rollbacks
**What is it?**
In real-world reservation systems (e.g., ticket booking, hotel rooms), resources are locked temporarily under a lease (e.g., 10-minute hold). If payment does not complete within the timeout, the lease expires and the resource is released via a compensating transaction.

**Why does it matter?**
Prevents abandoned user sessions or payment gateway delays from locking inventory indefinitely.

**Tiny C++ Example:**
```cpp
struct SeatHold {
    std::string seatId;
    std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point expiresAt;

    bool isExpired() const {
        return std::chrono::steady_clock::now() > expiresAt;
    }
};
```

**What should I notice?**
Timeouts must be evaluated against monotonic time (`std::chrono::steady_clock`), and expired holds must be safely returned to available inventory.

→ Practice implementing lease expirations and idempotent release logic in the following drill.

